Catalog main has predictive optimization enabled and schema main.ops has no explicit setting. An engineer previously disabled it on one table and now runs:
    ALTER TABLE main.ops.events INHERIT PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;
What is the effect on main.ops.events?

On a SQL warehouse, an engineer adds this statement to a job meant to pre-warm a dashboard:
    CACHE SELECT * FROM sales.gold.orders WHERE region = 'EU';
What happens?

An external Delta table has 50 columns. Queries filter on col_41, which has no statistics. The engineer runs:
    ALTER TABLE ext.events SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.dataSkippingStatsColumns' = 'event_date, col_41');
What else is needed before existing files can be skipped on col_41?

Table crm.contacts has this mask, and the analyst running the query is not in privacy_team:
    CREATE FUNCTION mask_email(e STRING) RETURNS STRING
      RETURN CASE WHEN is_account_group_member('privacy_team') THEN e ELSE '***' END;
    ALTER TABLE crm.contacts ALTER COLUMN email SET MASK mask_email;
    SELECT count(*) FROM crm.contacts WHERE email = 'ann@x.com';
A row with that email exists. What does the analyst get?

Column email in crm.contacts has the governed tag pii=email assigned. An engineer runs:
    ALTER TABLE crm.contacts DROP COLUMN email;
What happens?

Fine-grained write privileges are enabled. With USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT already in place, an admin runs:
